    "narrative": "The core of the LawDog lineup today is anchored by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who boasts our highest efficiency score of 76.64. His matchup against Tanner Gordon is an ideal pairing of park factors and a slow-delivery pitcher that creates a high-upside environment for his speed-driven upside. He is joined in the top tier by **Lawrence Butler** (70.57) and **Dylan Beavers** (69.52). Both outfielders benefit from strong platoon splits against Hayden Wesneski and Freddy Peralta respectively, with **Dylan Beavers** in particular deriving significant value from a combination of park-adjusted projections and his ability to exploit a pitcher susceptible to the stolen base. Meanwhile, **JJ Bleday** (68.50) secures a spot in the utility slot, buoyed by an exceptional +16% boost from Eduardo Rodriguez\u2019s unfavorable underlying metrics, making him a high-efficiency play despite the general volatility of his profile.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision today involved the exclusion of **Luis Arraez**, whose score of 55.71 hovered just below the threshold required to displace the current starters. While **Luis Arraez** offers a reliable floor, the optimizer prioritized the aggressive platoon edges and order-based bonuses afforded to the likes of **Gleyber Torres** (62.94) and **Daulton Varsho** (62.24). **Gleyber Torres** specifically benefits from a +13% dynamic platoon boost against Noah Cameron, rendering his higher ceiling more valuable for our 5x5 objectives than the contact-heavy profile of **Luis Arraez**. Conversely, **Justin Crawford** (52.74) remains on the bench due to a poor lineup position projection, which continues to cap his utility despite his elite sprint speed. Managers should keep a close watch on **Braden Montgomery**; while his 42.66 score suggests he is a fringe start, the 37% rain risk in his contest against Sean Manaea makes him a high-variance asset that we are comfortable avoiding in favor of our confirmed high-efficiency starters.",
